{"body": "I find one supported defect in J, and I will keep it separate from weaker or merely proposed worries.\n\nJ says:\n\n> “P9’s compatibility objection … is a criticism of a global aggregation/projection rule and of an independence assumption.”\n\nand:\n\n> “The compatibility objection targets the assumption that `R` is universal or irrelevant.”\n\nBut the Lean-compatible schematic fixes the target by these definitions:\n\n```lean\ndef CorrelationLoss ... : Prop :=\n  ∃ q, PossibleTotal ... q ∧\n       ¬ FeasibleTotal ... q\n\ndef IndependenceAssumption ... : Prop :=\n  ¬ CorrelationLoss ...\n```\n\nThat is not equivalent to “`R` is universal or irrelevant.” `CorrelationLoss` only says that some *total* `q` is locally possible but not feasible. Since `FeasibleTotal` is\n\n```lean\n∃ c, LocalChoice ... c ∧ R c ∧ Total c = q\n```\n\na total can be feasible through a different global choice even when the choice that produced it is excluded by `R`. So `IndependenceAssumption` can hold while `R` is non-universal.\n\nA distinguishing case: let bookings `A`, `B`, `C` have local alternatives\n\n```text\npossible_reservations A = [0, 10]\npossible_reservations B = [0, 10]\npossible_reservations C = [0, 20]\n```\n\nlet `Total c = c A + c B + c C`, and let `R` exclude only the global choice `(10, 10, 0)`. Then `R` is not universal. But every possible total `{0, 10, 20, 30, 40}` is still feasible: total `20` is produced by the excluded choice `(10, 10, 0)`, but also by `(0, 0, 20)`, which `R` permits. Hence `CorrelationLoss` is false, so `IndependenceAssumption` is true. Yet P9’s alleged incompatibility is present: the high-high combination is forbidden.\n\nP9’s actual sentence is:\n\n> “intervals lose correlations: booking A’s high alternative might be incompatible with booking B’s high alternative if they share a physical resource not named in the snapshot.”\n\nThat is a point about combinations of choices, not only about totals. J’s `CorrelationLoss` sees only totals. So J has lost the distinction between combination-level compatibility and total-level feasibility. Its theorem\n\n```lean\ntheorem correlation_loss_refutes_exact_independent ...\n```\n\nis valid, but it refutes exactness only when the correlation loss survives projection to totals. It does not support J’s broader claim that P9 targets `IndependenceAssumption` as J defines it.\n\nThis is a defect in J’s added artifact, not a defect in WHL’s inability to model resources. E009’s criticism that WHL lacks correlation and non-convexity primitives remains admissible. J’s prose escape path can still say the combination-level point, but then the Lean carrier does not carry that point; it carries a weaker total-level condition.\n\nA secondary, more tentative concern: J’s `IndependentClaim` only says that a reported set `S` contains every locally possible total. It does not formalize the independent per-booking extrema or interval aggregation itself. So the link from the theorem back to “the aggregation rule” is not fully carried by the schematic. That may be a sketch limitation rather than an established defect.\n\nSo I would retain J’s addition of `R`, `PossibleTotal`, `FeasibleTotal`, and the exact/over-approximate distinction as a proposal, but I would not accept its identification of `IndependenceAssumption` with “`R` is universal or irrelevant.” At minimum, `CorrelationLoss` would need to concern global choices or combinations, not only totals, for J’s stated P7/P9 target to be the target its formal carrier actually represents.", "commitments": "I find one supported defect in J, and I will keep it separate from weaker or merely proposed worries.\n\nJ says:\n\n> “P9’s compatibility objection … is a criticism of a global aggregation/projection rule and of an independence assumption.”\n\nand:\n\n> “The compatibility objection targets the assumption that `R` is universal or irrelevant.”\n\nBut the Lean-compatible schematic fixes the target by these definitions:\n\n```lean\ndef CorrelationLoss ... : Prop :=\n  ∃ q, PossibleTotal ... q ∧\n       ¬ FeasibleTotal ... q\n\ndef IndependenceAssumption ... : Prop :=\n  ¬ CorrelationLoss ...\n```\n\nThat is not equivalent to “`R` is universal or irrelevant.” `CorrelationLoss` only says that some *total* `q` is locally possible but not feasible. Since `FeasibleTotal` is\n\n```lean\n∃ c, LocalChoice ... c ∧ R c ∧ Total c = q\n```\n\na total can be feasible through a different global choice even when the choice that produced it is excluded by `R`. So `IndependenceAssumption` can hold while `R` is non-universal.\n\nA distinguishing case: let bookings `A`, `B`, `C` have local alternatives\n\n```text\npossible_reservations A = [0, 10]\npossible_reservations B = [0, 10]\npossible_reservations C = [0, 20]\n```\n\nlet `Total c = c A + c B + c C`, and let `R` exclude only the global choice `(10, 10, 0)`. Then `R` is not universal. But every possible total `{0, 10, 20, 30, 40}` is still feasible: total `20` is produced by the excluded choice `(10, 10, 0)`, but also by `(0, 0, 20)`, which `R` permits. Hence `CorrelationLoss` is false, so `IndependenceAssumption` is true. Yet P9’s alleged incompatibility is present: the high-high combination is forbidden.\n\nP9’s actual sentence is:\n\n> “intervals lose correlations: booking A’s high alternative might be incompatible with booking B’s high alternative if they share a physical resource not named in the snapshot.”\n\nThat is a point about combinations of choices, not only about totals. J’s `CorrelationLoss` sees only totals. So J has lost the distinction between combination-level compatibility and total-level feasibility. Its theorem\n\n```lean\ntheorem correlation_loss_refutes_exact_independent ...\n```\n\nis valid, but it refutes exactness only when the correlation loss survives projection to totals. It does not support J’s broader claim that P9 targets `IndependenceAssumption` as J defines it.\n\nThis is a defect in J’s added artifact, not a defect in WHL’s inability to model resources. E009’s criticism that WHL lacks correlation and non-convexity primitives remains admissible. J’s prose escape path can still say the combination-level point, but then the Lean carrier does not carry that point; it carries a weaker total-level condition.\n\nA secondary, more tentative concern: J’s `IndependentClaim` only says that a reported set `S` contains every locally possible total. It does not formalize the independent per-booking extrema or interval aggregation itself. So the link from the theorem back to “the aggregation rule” is not fully carried by the schematic. That may be a sketch limitation rather than an established defect.\n\nSo I would retain J’s addition of `R`, `PossibleTotal`, `FeasibleTotal`, and the exact/over-approximate distinction as a proposal, but I would not accept its identification of `IndependenceAssumption` with “`R` is universal or irrelevant.” At minimum, `CorrelationLoss` would need to concern global choices or combinations, not only totals, for J’s stated P7/P9 target to be the target its formal carrier actually represents."}